/* Write the week data files for weeks 11-20, on the CHAINED-shot shape.
 *
 *   node scripts/week-videos/_generate-week-files-v2.mjs
 *
 * Weeks 1-10 are generated by _generate-week-files.mjs on a two-shot shape
 * and are shipped. This file is the next shape, and exists because of one
 * piece of owner feedback, 2026-10-29:
 *
 *   "I'm not too keen on the slow motion side of things. Where when it shows
 *    Adam and Layla is too slow motion, so coordinate all properly, maybe
 *    speed up their motion a little bit, but then I want you to still get the
 *    narration in there."
 *
 * ── WHY THE FILMS CAME OUT IN SLOW MOTION ────────────────────────────────
 * Two causes, and the prompt wording was only the smaller one.
 *
 * 1. Every one of the forty clips was ordered with the words "Slow, smooth
 *    cinematic shot." That is now motionBrisk() in the prompt kit.
 *
 * 2. The real cause: a clip is ordered as long as the voice laid over it
 *    needs, and a two-shot film puts THREE lines on its opening shot, so that
 *    shot was ordered at 10 to 15 seconds. Across those fifteen seconds the
 *    pinned pair of frames differed by one small gesture - a mask moved away
 *    from a face. One gesture spread over fifteen seconds is slow motion, and
 *    no prompt wording survives it. The fix is not wording, it is to stop
 *    ordering long clips.
 *
 * ── CHAINED SHOTS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * So a shot carries at most two lines, and when a beat needs more than about
 * eight seconds it becomes TWO shots that chain: the second shot's start
 * frame IS the first shot's end frame, the same picture, declared with
 * `chain: true`. That costs one clip and one frame rather than two frames,
 * the join is seamless because both sides are literally the same image, and
 * each order covers a short distance instead of a long one.
 *
 * Chaining also removes the fault that hit week 7, where the outro showed a
 * different machine from its own intro: a shot that begins on the previous
 * shot's last frame cannot drift off the set.
 *
 * `floor` lets a shot be ordered a little longer than its voice strictly
 * needs, which is the other half of the owner's note: the film may run
 * slightly longer, it just may not drift. `move` describes TWO beats rather
 * than one gesture, so a brisk order has enough to do.
 *
 * ── EVERY LINE IS ATTACHED TO ITS OWN SHOT ───────────────────────────────
 * `say` sits on the shot, so a line cannot be mis-assigned to a picture it
 * does not belong to, and the voice is stated rather than guessed from the
 * text. The Raccoon only ever speaks on a shot he is alone in, which is also
 * what the owner asked for first: "Make it clear when the video is speaking
 * and when 11 Labs is speaking."
 *
 * CHILD SAFETY, and week 11 is the week it matters most. The film never shows
 * the bad thing happening to a child, and never shows a child frightened.
 * Week 11's subject is telling a trusted grown-up, so the adversary in the
 * picture is SECRECY: the Raccoon smothering an emergency beacon lamp so nobody sees
 * it. The heroes' answer is to light a lamp and have three lights answer
 * back. No charity or helpline is named or shown, per the owner's standing
 * rule that it is "the child line service" and never a named organisation.
 */
import { writeFileSync } from "node:fs";

import {
  villain,
  heroes,
  KEEP_VILLAIN_V2 as KEEP_VILLAIN,
  KEEP_HEROES,
  endFrame,
  motionBrisk,
} from "./_prompt-kit.mjs";

/* ── BIG CHANGES, NOT SHORT CLIPS ────────────────────────────────────────
 * The first cut of week 11 was ordered on the theory that duration was the
 * problem, and the rendered clips disproved it. What actually governs whether
 * a pinned pair reads as brisk is how much VISIBLE CHANGE sits between the
 * two frames, measured against the time the clip runs:
 *
 *   4s, wide shot to a close-up of his face      brisk, purposeful     KEEP
 *   6s, blanket thrown off a blazing lamp        brisk                 KEEP
 *   6s, three lights appear, both arms point     brisk                 KEEP
 *   4s, paws come down and shoulders slump       reads fine            KEEP
 *   7s, a lever turns a little, camera creeps    drift, warped face  REJECT
 *   8s, a blanket is wound slightly tighter      aimless wandering   REJECT
 *
 * The model does not slow an action down to fill the time: given a long order
 * and nothing much to do, it invents drift, and on the 7s hero shot it
 * distorted Layla's face at 2.3s. Both rejects were small deltas. Both of the
 * longest keepers were big ones.
 *
 * So the rule for a shot is NOT "keep it short". It is: name ONE physical
 * action with an unmistakable before and after - a light going on or out, a
 * thing crossing the frame, a camera moving from wide to close - and give the
 * shot ONE line of voice. If a beat needs two lines, it is two shots that
 * chain. SLOW_RISK below is a backstop for the time side of that, not the
 * whole rule; a shot under it with nothing to do will still wander.
 */

/** Past this many seconds even a decent delta starts to thin out. */
const SLOW_RISK = 8;

const EMMA = "EMMA";
const RACCOON = "CALLUM";

/* ── the weeks ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
const WEEKS = [
  {
    w: 11,
    title: "Something Wrong? Emergency Protocol",
    /* Week 10 already wears lime green mountain rescue jackets, so the
     * emergency week cannot be a rescue theme. Lighthouse keepers instead:
     * when something is wrong you light the lamp, and your team answers. */
    costume:
      "deep navy lighthouse keepers' coats with brass buttons and knitted amber scarves",
    den: "a dark cluttered junk den lit by a cold teal glow, full of old machines and cables",
    denLight: "Deep shadows, teal and warm amber rim light.",
    world:
      "the round glass lamp room at the top of a lighthouse at night, with a huge polished brass lamp mechanism, a big brass lever on its side and a curved glass wall looking out over a dark sea",
    worldLight: "Warm amber lamplight against deep blue night.",

    /* FIVE shots, one line each. The first cut of this intro had three, and
     * two of them came back unusable for the reason in BIG CHANGES above: an
     * 8s shot whose pair differed by "the blanket is wound a bit tighter",
     * and a 7s shot whose pair differed by "the lever has turned a little".
     * Both wandered. The outro's shots, whose pairs differ by a light going
     * on or three lights appearing, all came back clean at 4s and 6s. */
    intro: [
      {
        who: "villain",
        floor: 4,
        beat: "He throws the blanket over the beacon.",
        pose: "standing over an amber beacon lamp that is UNCOVERED on the floor and blazing brightly, flooding the whole den with warm golden light, holding a heavy grey blanket up spread wide between both paws above his head, about to throw it down over the lamp",
        end: "the heavy grey blanket has been thrown right over the beacon lamp and hangs over it, and he is leaning over the covered lamp with both paws on it: the den has gone dark and teal again with only a muffled amber glow under the blanket",
        move: "The raccoon throws the grey blanket down over the blazing beacon lamp and leans onto it with both paws, and the den drops from warm gold to dark teal as the light is smothered.",
        say: [[EMMA, "Sometimes something online feels WRONG."]],
      },
      {
        who: "villain",
        chain: true,
        floor: 5,
        beat: "He winds it tight.",
        end: "the heavy grey blanket is wound tightly all the way round the beacon lamp so that only a thin sliver of amber light escapes it, and he is pressing the last fold down flat with both paws",
        move: "The raccoon winds the heavy grey blanket round and round the covered lamp with quick busy paws, then presses the last fold down flat, squeezing the glow down to a thin sliver.",
        say: [[EMMA, "And the Raccoon's best trick is making you keep it secret."]],
      },
      {
        who: "villain",
        chain: true,
        floor: 4,
        beat: "His gloat, and the last of the light goes out.",
        end: "he has leaned in much closer to the camera, filling more of the frame, grinning widely with one claw raised to his lips, and the thin sliver of amber light under the blanket has gone out completely",
        move: "The raccoon leans in fast towards the camera and raises one claw to his lips, grinning, and the last sliver of amber light under the blanket goes out.",
        say: [[RACCOON, "[whispers] Shhh. Nobody needs to know."]],
      },
      {
        who: "heroes",
        floor: 4,
        beat: "They reach the lamp and take the lever.",
        pose: "striding together towards the huge brass lamp mechanism, both of them reaching out for the big brass lever on its side",
        end: "both children have arrived at the lamp and have all four of their hands gripped together around the big brass lever, which is still pointing UP, braced to pull with their feet planted wide and their knees bent",
        move: "The two children cross to the brass lamp in a few quick strides, get all four hands onto the big lever together and brace themselves against it.",
        say: [[EMMA, "Not a chance, Raccoon."]],
      },
      {
        who: "heroes",
        chain: true,
        floor: 5,
        beat: "The lamp blazes.",
        end: "the big brass lever has been thrown fully DOWN so that it points at the floor, both children have been carried down with it into a low crouch, and the huge lamp has blazed into brilliant white-gold light throwing a powerful beam right out through the curved glass",
        move: "The two children haul the big lever all the way down into a crouch, and the lamp bursts into brilliant white-gold light, throwing a powerful beam out across the sea.",
        say: [[EMMA, "This week we learn who to TELL, and how fast."]],
      },
    ],

    outro: [
      {
        who: "villain",
        floor: 6,
        beat: "The beacon breaks out, and the den floods with light.",
        pose: "staggering back from the heavy grey blanket on the floor, which has burst open with brilliant amber light blazing out of it, throwing up one paw to shield his eyes",
        end: "the grey blanket has been thrown right off and lies in a heap, the amber beacon lamp blazes on the floor flooding the whole den with warm light, and he has backed away against a stack of old machines with both paws up",
        move: "The grey blanket is thrown right off the blazing amber beacon lamp, warm light floods across the whole den, and the raccoon backs away fast against a stack of old machines with both paws up.",
        say: [[EMMA, "He tried so hard to keep you QUIET."]],
      },
      {
        who: "villain",
        chain: true,
        floor: 4,
        beat: "Beaten, in the light he tried to smother.",
        end: "his paws have come down from his face and his arms hang at his sides, his shoulders and ears have drooped right down in defeat, and he is standing in the full flood of warm amber light with the discarded grey blanket at his feet",
        move: "The raccoon's paws come down from his face, his arms drop to his sides and his shoulders and ears droop right down in defeat, in the full flood of warm amber light.",
        say: [[RACCOON, "You TOLD someone?!"]],
      },
      /* The celebration is the shot the owner named. On the two-shot shape it
       * carried both closing lines and would have been ordered at NINE
       * seconds, so it is two chained shots: the beam goes out, then the
       * lights answer. Each one is brisk, and the two lines now land on
       * different pictures, which is the better film anyway. */
      {
        who: "heroes",
        floor: 4,
        beat: "The beam goes out across the sea.",
        pose: "standing side by side at the curved glass wall of the lamp room, with the bright amber beam just beginning to sweep out over the dark sea in front of them, both smiling",
        end: "the bright amber beam has swept right out across the dark sea and reaches far away into the distance, and both children are watching it go with big open smiles",
        move: "The two children turn to the glass and look up as the bright amber beam brightens and stretches further out across the dark sea.",
        say: [[EMMA, "[proud] You told someone. Straight away."]],
      },
      {
        who: "heroes",
        chain: true,
        floor: 6,
        beat: "Three lights answer.",
        end: "three small warm amber lights have come on far out in the dark sea answering the beam, and both children are pointing out at them with big open smiles",
        move: "Three small warm amber lights come on one after another far out in the dark sea, answering the beam, and the two children both lift their arms and point out at them, smiling.",
        /* Kept short on purpose. The recording is not deterministic: this
         * line came back at 4.6s on one take and 6.5s on the next, and the
         * shot is ordered against whatever it measures, so a wordy closer
         * can force a nine-second order off a slow read alone. */
        say: [[EMMA, "Never your fault, Cyber Hero. Week eleven, done."]],
      },
    ],
  },
];

/* ── emit ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
for (const k of WEEKS) {
  const ww = String(k.w).padStart(2, "0");
  let risky = 0;

  /* Which of the intro's frames establishes each world, so the outro can be
   * generated from it instead of re-describing it. Filled once the intro is
   * built, and read only while building the outro. */
  let introShots = null;
  const introSetFrame = (isVillain) => {
    const s = isVillain
      ? introShots.find((x) => x.villain && !x.startFrom)
      : introShots.find((x) => !x.villain && !x.startFrom);
    if (!s) throw new Error(`the intro has no ${isVillain ? "villain" : "hero"} shot to take a set from`);
    /* Its LAST frame, so the outro opens on the world as the intro left it. */
    return `${s.file}-end`;
  };

  const build = (tag, specs) =>
    specs.map((spec, i) => {
      const file = `${tag}-shot${i + 1}`;
      const isVillain = spec.who === "villain";
      if (spec.chain && i === 0) throw new Error(`${file}: the first shot cannot chain`);
      if (!spec.chain && !spec.pose) throw new Error(`${file}: an unchained shot needs a pose`);
      /* The Raccoon may only be heard over a shot he is alone in. */
      for (const [v] of spec.say) {
        if (v === RACCOON && !isVillain) throw new Error(`${file}: the Raccoon speaks over a hero shot`);
      }
      if (spec.floor > SLOW_RISK) {
        console.warn(`  ! w${ww} ${file} floor ${spec.floor}s is over ${SLOW_RISK}s: slow-motion risk`);
        risky++;
      }
      return {
        file,
        villain: isVillain,
        floor: spec.floor,
        beat: spec.beat,
        say: spec.say,
        startFrom: spec.chain ? `${tag}-shot${i}-end` : null,
        /* The OUTRO's first shot is generated from the INTRO's last frame of
         * the same world, not from a character sprite. Weeks 1-10 built it
         * from the `raccoon-hurt` sprite, and on week 11 that came back with
         * GREEN eyes against the amber eyes of the intro - the same class of
         * fault as week 7's outro showing a different machine. Starting from
         * the intro's own frame makes the set, the lighting and the character
         * identical by construction rather than by description. */
        startRefs: spec.chain
          ? null
          : tag === "outro"
            ? [introSetFrame(isVillain)]
            : isVillain
              ? ["raccoon-idle"]
              : ["adam", "layla"],
        /* An intro shot describes its world from scratch. An outro shot is a
         * CHANGE to the frame the intro left that world on, which is why it
         * reads as an end-frame prompt rather than a scene description. */
        start: spec.chain
          ? null
          : tag === "outro"
            ? endFrame(spec.pose, isVillain ? KEEP_VILLAIN : KEEP_HEROES(k.costume))
            : isVillain
              ? villain({ pose: spec.pose, set: k.den, light: k.denLight })
              : heroes({ costume: k.costume, pose: spec.pose, set: k.world, light: k.worldLight }),
        end: endFrame(spec.end, isVillain ? KEEP_VILLAIN : KEEP_HEROES(k.costume)),
        move: motionBrisk(spec.move),
      };
    });

  const intro = build("intro", k.intro);
  introShots = intro;
  const outro = build("outro", k.outro);

  /* The polaroid on the screen after the intro is cut from the GLOAT shot,
   * where the Raccoon's line has landed: that is the picture the words "oh
   * no, look what happened" belong to. */
  const gloat = intro.findLast((s) => s.say.some(([v]) => v === RACCOON));
  if (!gloat) throw new Error("the intro has no Raccoon line to cut the alert photo from");

  const shotLines = (shots) =>
    shots
      .map(
        (s) =>
          `      { file: ${JSON.stringify(s.file)}, seconds: ${s.floor}, beat: ${JSON.stringify(s.beat)}${
            s.startFrom ? `, startFrom: ${JSON.stringify(s.startFrom)}` : ""
          } },`
      )
      .join("\n");

  const voiceLines = (tag, shots) => {
    let n = 0;
    return shots
      .flatMap((s, i) =>
        s.say.map(
          ([v, t]) =>
            `      { file: ${JSON.stringify(`${tag}-${++n}`)}, shot: ${i + 1}, voice: ${JSON.stringify(v)}, text: ${JSON.stringify(t)} },`
        )
      )
      .join("\n");
  };

  const frameLines = (shots) =>
    shots
      .flatMap((s) => {
        const out = [];
        if (s.startFrom) {
          out.push(`  /* ${s.file} starts on ${s.startFrom}, the same picture: no frame of its own. */`);
        } else {
          out.push(
            `  { file: ${JSON.stringify(`${s.file}-start`)}, refs: ${JSON.stringify(s.startRefs)}, prompt: ${JSON.stringify(s.start)} },`
          );
        }
        out.push(`  { file: ${JSON.stringify(`${s.file}-end`)}, fromPrevious: true, prompt: ${JSON.stringify(s.end)} },`);
        return out;
      })
      .join("\n");

  const motionLines = (shots) =>
    shots.map((s) => `  ${JSON.stringify(s.file)}: ${JSON.stringify(s.move)},`).join("\n");

  const file = `/* WEEK ${k.w} bookend videos - "${k.title}".
 *
 * Generated by scripts/week-videos/_generate-week-files-v2.mjs, the chained-shot
 * shape. Edit there, not here, unless this one week needs to differ.
 *
 * No line carries a start time. The build measures each recorded mp3 and lays
 * the lines end to end with a gap, so two voices CANNOT overlap: that was
 * week 1's fault. Every line NAMES the shot it is spoken over, and the build
 * holds a shot on its last frame until its own lines have finished, so a line
 * cannot run past its own picture either.
 *
 * A shot carries at most two lines, so no clip is ordered longer than about
 * eight seconds: past that a pinned pair reads as slow motion, which is what
 * the owner saw in weeks 1-10. \`seconds\` is a FLOOR, not a target - the
 * length asked of the video model is the larger of it and what the voice over
 * that shot needs.
 *
 * A shot with \`startFrom\` has no start frame of its own: it begins on the
 * previous shot's END frame, the same picture, so the two clips chain with no
 * seam and neither has to cover much distance.
 */

export const WEEK = ${k.w};
export const TITLE = ${JSON.stringify(k.title)};

/* The incident-report polaroid on the screen after the intro is cut from this
 * shot, where the Raccoon's line lands. On the two-shot weeks it was shot 1. */
export const ALERT_SHOT = ${JSON.stringify(gloat.file)};

export const VIDEOS = [
  {
    name: "module-${ww}-intro",
    shots: [
${shotLines(intro)}
    ],
    lines: [
${voiceLines("intro", intro)}
    ],
  },
  {
    name: "module-${ww}-outro",
    shots: [
${shotLines(outro)}
    ],
    lines: [
${voiceLines("outro", outro)}
    ],
  },
];

/* Each shot is pinned at BOTH ends, so the video model interpolates between
 * two pictures of one set instead of inventing its way out of one. */
export const FRAMES = [
${frameLines(intro)}
${frameLines(outro)}
];

/* What moves between the two pinned frames. TWO beats per shot, so a brisk
 * order has enough to do across its whole length. */
export const MOTION = {
${motionLines(intro)}
${motionLines(outro)}
};
`;

  writeFileSync(`scripts/week-videos/w${ww}.mjs`, file);
  const shots = [...intro, ...outro];
  const clips = shots.length;
  const frames = shots.filter((s) => !s.startFrom).length + clips;
  console.log(`w${ww}.mjs  ${k.title}`);
  console.log(
    `         ${clips} clips, ${frames} frames  (~${clips * 600 + frames * 40} credits)  alert photo from ${gloat.file}  ${risky} slow-motion risk(s)`
  );
}
